<article class="w-full my-12 text-neutral-800" aria-labelledby="guide-heading">
  <!-- Article Header -->
  <div class="border-b border-neutral-200/80 pb-6 mb-8">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 text-xs font-bold mb-3">
      <span>📚 The Definitive Protractor & Geometry Masterclass</span>
    </div>
    <h2 id="guide-heading" class="text-2xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-neutral-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
      What is a Protractor, How to Use It, and How to Measure Angles Online
    </h2>
    <p class="text-neutral-500 text-sm sm:text-base mt-2 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
      Whether you are a 4th-grade elementary student completing your first geometry homework, a high schooler practicing with a <strong>measuring angles with a protractor worksheet</strong>, a teacher creating lesson plans, or a carpenter double-checking miter cuts, this comprehensive guide will teach you everything you need to know about protractors, angles, and geometry tools.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: What is a Protractor & What Does It Look Like? -->
  <section id="what-is-a-protractor" class="mb-12">
    <div class="bg-white border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>🔍 What is a Protractor (and What Does a Protractor Look Like)?</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>What is a protractor?</strong> In geometry and drafting, a <strong>protractor</strong> is a specialized instrument calibrated to measure, calculate, and construct angles in units of degrees (symbol: °) and radians. Physical protractors are most commonly manufactured from transparent acrylic plastic, polished brass, stainless steel, or wood.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong>What does a protractor look like?</strong> The classic physical protractor is semi-circular (a 180° protractor) resembling a sliced circle with a flat bottom edge and a curved upper dome. Key anatomy includes:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-6">
        <div class="bg-[#faf9f8] border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <span class="text-lg mb-1 block font-bold text-blue-600">1. Baseline & Origin Hole</span>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600">The straight bottom ruler marked with centimeter/inch increments. Exactly at its center sits the <em>origin crosshair (vertex hole)</em>, which must sit directly on top of the angle's vertex.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#faf9f8] border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <span class="text-lg mb-1 block font-bold text-emerald-600">2. Dual Degree Scales</span>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600">Two concentric semicircular scales: an <strong>outer scale</strong> reading from 0° on the right to 180° on the left, and an <strong>inner scale</strong> reading from 0° on the left to 180° on the right.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#faf9f8] border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <span class="text-lg mb-1 block font-bold text-purple-600">3. Precision Degree Ticks</span>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600">Fine 1° tick marks along the outer perimeter, medium 5° intermediate lines, and bold 10° divisions marked with clear numerical values.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
        Beyond standard physical tools, a <strong>digital protractor</strong> (such as <strong>TheOnlineProtractor.com</strong>) runs directly inside your web browser. A virtual <strong>online protractor tool</strong> offers significant advantages over plastic tools: you can zoom in with a 2.5x magnifying loupe, snap automatically to exact degrees, upload photos or math worksheets, and export annotated images without needing a physical ruler or compass.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: What is a Protractor Used For? -->
  <section id="what-is-a-protractor-used-for" class="mb-12">
    <div class="bg-white border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>📐 What is a Protractor Used For in School, Work, and Daily Life?</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The primary purpose of a <strong>protractor tool</strong> is angle quantification. Here are the most prominent everyday and professional applications:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
        <div class="p-4 border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl bg-neutral-50/60">
          <div class="text-2xl mb-2">🎒</div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-sm text-neutral-900 mb-1">School Mathematics</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Elementary and middle school geometry curricula (Grade 4–8) introduce protractors to teach acute, right, obtuse, straight, and reflex angle concepts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl bg-neutral-50/60">
          <div class="text-2xl mb-2">🏗️</div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-sm text-neutral-900 mb-1">Architecture & Engineering</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Draftsmen and structural engineers use digital protractor tools to verify roof pitches, truss bevels, staircase inclines, and CAD diagrams.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl bg-neutral-50/60">
          <div class="text-2xl mb-2">🪚</div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-sm text-neutral-900 mb-1">Carpentry & Woodworking</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Woodworkers measure precise bevels and miter saw blade angles (e.g. 45° corner frames or 22.5° octagonal joints) to ensure seamless joints.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl bg-neutral-50/60">
          <div class="text-2xl mb-2">🧭</div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-sm text-neutral-900 mb-1">Navigation & Astronomy</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Navigators utilize 360° circular protractor tools to calculate nautical bearings, true north compass courses, and celestial elevations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: How to Use a Protractor to Measure Angles (Step by Step) -->
  <section id="how-to-use-a-protractor" class="mb-12">
    <div class="bg-white border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>📖 How to Use a Protractor to Measure Angles (How to Use Protractor Step-by-Step)</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Measuring an angle requires aligning two rays that meet at a shared point called the <strong>vertex</strong>. Follow these proven steps to achieve 100% accuracy every time:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <!-- Step 1 -->
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 p-5 bg-[#faf9f8] border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-xl">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-orange-500 text-white font-black text-base flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 shadow-sm">
            1
          </div>
          <div class="flex-1">
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-1">
              Step 1: Place the Vertex Hole (Orange Handle <span class="text-orange-600">V</span>)
            </h4>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Find the point where the two rays or line segments meet (the vertex of the angle). Place the center origin hole of your physical protractor directly over this corner point. On <strong>TheOnlineProtractor.com</strong>, simply drag the bright orange circular pin <strong class="text-orange-600">V</strong> to the corner. The automatic 2.5x magnifying loupe will pop up to show you an enlarged crosshair view for sub-pixel accuracy.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 2 -->
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 p-5 bg-[#faf9f8] border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-xl">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-blue-600 text-white font-black text-base flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 shadow-sm">
            2
          </div>
          <div class="flex-1">
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-1">
              Step 2: Align the Baseline 0° Ray (Blue Handle <span class="text-blue-600">A</span>)
            </h4>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Rotate the protractor so that the straight baseline edge lies perfectly flat along one of the angle's arms (the base ray). This established arm serves as your 0° starting baseline. On our digital tool, drag the bright blue handle <strong class="text-blue-600">A</strong> anywhere along this bottom line.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 3 -->
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 p-5 bg-[#faf9f8] border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-xl">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-emerald-600 text-white font-black text-base flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 shadow-sm">
            3
          </div>
          <div class="flex-1">
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-1">
              Step 3: Read the Degree Scale Along the Second Ray (Green Handle <span class="text-emerald-600">B</span>)
            </h4>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
              Track the second line (the terminal ray) as it extends out from the vertex through the curved outer arc. Read the number where the line crosses the tick marks. On <strong>TheOnlineProtractor.com</strong>, drag the emerald green handle <strong class="text-emerald-600">B</strong> onto this second ray, and the exact digital angle, angle name (Acute, Right, Obtuse, Reflex), and radians will display immediately in the top readout!
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: How to Read a Protractor (Inner vs Outer Scales) -->
  <section id="how-to-read-a-protractor" class="mb-12">
    <div class="bg-white border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>🔄 How to Read a Protractor: Understanding Inner vs Outer Scales</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        One of the most common mistakes students make on math homework and tests is reading the wrong scale on a protractor (for example, reading 140° instead of 40°). Why are there two rows of numbers stamped on a protractor?
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-4">
        <div class="bg-blue-50/50 border border-blue-200/80 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-blue-900 text-sm mb-2">Outer Scale (Right to Left / Counter-Clockwise)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed mb-2">
            The <strong>outer scale</strong> starts at <strong>0° on the right side</strong> and counts upwards to 180° on the left side.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed font-semibold">
            👉 When to use: Use the outer scale when your angle opens up and to the left (counter-clockwise) starting from a baseline pointing to the right.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-emerald-50/50 border border-emerald-200/80 rounded-xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-emerald-900 text-sm mb-2">Inner Scale (Left to Right / Clockwise)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed mb-2">
            The <strong>inner scale</strong> starts at <strong>0° on the left side</strong> and counts upwards to 180° on the right side.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed font-semibold">
            👉 When to use: Use the inner scale when your angle opens up and to the right (clockwise) starting from a baseline pointing to the left.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl text-xs text-amber-900">
        <strong>💡 Pro-Tip for Students:</strong> Always check if your angle looks sharp (acute, &lt;90°) or wide (obtuse, &gt;90°) before writing down your answer. If your angle is sharp like a slice of pizza, but your reading says 130°, you read the wrong scale! The correct answer is 180° - 130° = 50°.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: How to Measure Angles Without a Protractor -->
  <section id="without-protractor" class="mb-12">
    <div class="bg-white border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>🛠️ How to Measure Angles Without a Protractor</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Need to measure an angle immediately but don't own a plastic protractor? Here are 4 reliable methods to measure angles without a physical protractor:
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#faf9f8]">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-sm mb-1">Method 1: Use TheOnlineProtractor.com (Fastest & Most Accurate)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            Take a quick smartphone picture or screen capture of the object, worksheet, or diagram. Open <strong>TheOnlineProtractor.com</strong> and press <kbd class="px-1 bg-neutral-200 rounded font-mono">Ctrl+V</kbd> to paste it. Drag the digital pins over the photo to get a laser-accurate 0.1° readout immediately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#faf9f8]">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-sm mb-1">Method 2: Origami Paper Corner Folding</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            Every standard sheet of printer or notebook paper has exact 90° right angles at its four corners. Fold a corner diagonally corner-to-edge to create a precise 45° angle reference. Fold in half again for 22.5°.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#faf9f8]">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-sm mb-1">Method 3: Trigonometry (Tangent Formula)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            Construct a right triangle with a standard ruler along the angle rays. Measure the length of the opposite side ($O$) and adjacent side ($A$). Calculate the angle using: $\theta = \arctan(O / A) \times (180 / \pi)$.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-neutral-200 rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#faf9f8]">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-sm mb-1">Method 4: Clock Face Analogy</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            A 12-hour clock face consists of 360°, meaning each hour number equals exactly 30° ($360^\circ \div 12 = 30^\circ$). Each minute tick mark equals 6° ($360^\circ \div 60 = 6^\circ$).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 6: Protractor and Compass Constructions -->
  <section id="protractor-and-compass" class="mb-12">
    <div class="bg-white border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>✏️ Protractor and Compass: The Classic Geometry Toolkit</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        In traditional Euclidean geometry, a <strong>protractor and compass</strong> work together to solve construction problems:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-neutral-700">
        <li><strong>Constructing Regular Polygons:</strong> Use the protractor to calculate interior angles (e.g. 60° for equilateral triangles, 108° for regular pentagons, 120° for hexagons) and use the compass to mark equal side lengths along each ray.</li>
        <li><strong>Bisecting Angles:</strong> A compass draws intersecting arcs from equal radii along both rays; the line connecting the vertex to the arc intersection bisects the angle into two equal halves, which can be verified with a protractor.</li>
        <li><strong>Drawing Perpendicular Bisectors:</strong> Use the compass to find equidistant arc intersections above and below a segment, creating a perfect 90° right angle verified by the protractor.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 7: Types of Angles Master Reference -->
  <section id="types-of-angles" class="mb-8">
    <div class="border border-neutral-200/80 rounded-2xl bg-white p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>📊 The 6 Types of Angles (Flashcards & Real-World Examples)</span>
      </h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <!-- Acute -->
        <div class="border border-sky-200 bg-sky-50/40 rounded-xl p-4.5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
            <span class="text-3xl">🍕</span>
            <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-bold bg-sky-100 text-sky-800">0° to 89.9°</span>
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-base mb-1">Acute Angle</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            Sharp angle smaller than 90°. Examples: a slice of pizza, hands of a clock at 2:00, or opened scissors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Right -->
        <div class="border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50/40 rounded-xl p-4.5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
            <span class="text-3xl">📐</span>
            <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-bold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800">Exact 90.0°</span>
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-base mb-1">Right Angle</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            Forms an exact square "L" corner. Examples: corners of books, computer screens, doorway edges, and graph paper grids.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Obtuse -->
        <div class="border border-orange-200 bg-orange-50/40 rounded-xl p-4.5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
            <span class="text-3xl">💻</span>
            <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-bold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800">90.1° to 179.9°</span>
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-base mb-1">Obtuse Angle</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            Wide angle between 90° and 180°. Examples: an open laptop screen, reclining chair, or hands of a clock at 5:00.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Straight -->
        <div class="border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50/40 rounded-xl p-4.5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
            <span class="text-3xl">📏</span>
            <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-bold bg-amber-100 text-amber-800">Exact 180.0°</span>
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-base mb-1">Straight Angle</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            A completely flat straight line. Examples: an open wooden ruler laid flat, hands on a clock at 6:00, or the flat horizon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Reflex -->
        <div class="border border-purple-200 bg-purple-50/40 rounded-xl p-4.5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
            <span class="text-3xl">🟡</span>
            <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-bold bg-purple-100 text-purple-800">180.1° to 359.9°</span>
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-base mb-1">Reflex Angle</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            A large angle greater than a straight line. Examples: Pac-Man opening his mouth wide, or hands on a clock at 8:00.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Full Circle -->
        <div class="border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50/40 rounded-xl p-4.5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
            <span class="text-3xl">🎡</span>
            <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-bold bg-indigo-100 text-indigo-800">Exact 360.0°</span>
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-neutral-900 text-base mb-1">Full Rotation</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">
            A complete circle all the way around. Examples: a full turn of a bicycle wheel, spinning ferris wheel, or 360° skateboard spin.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</article>
